WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.667 --> 00:00:18.799
People say that the dog is man's best friend.

2
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.249
They say this because dogs are loyal and loving pets.

3
00:00:24.250 --> 00:00:29.794
Unfortunately, man is not always a good friend to dogs.

4
00:00:29.795 --> 00:00:35.896
This was the case with Patrasche and his master.

5
00:00:35.897 --> 00:00:42.915
Patrasche was a beautiful, sandy-colored working dog from Flanders in Belgium.

6
00:00:42.916 --> 00:00:48.700
Dogs from Flanders were famous because they were so strong.

7
00:00:48.701 --> 00:00:56.516
They could pull heavy carts and work hard for hours, like small horses.

8
00:00:56.517 --> 00:01:02.345
When Patrasche was still a puppy, he was sold to a merchant.

9
00:01:02.346 --> 00:01:07.102
His new master sold pots and pans for a living.

10
00:01:07.103 --> 00:01:16.783
He traveled all over Belgium with his cart of goods, from the blue sea to the green mountains.

11
00:01:16.784 --> 00:01:22.895
Patrasche's master was a drunkard and a fat, mean man.

12
00:01:22.896 --> 00:01:30.522
He piled his cart high with pans, buckets, jars, brass tins, and cooking pots.

13
00:01:30.523 --> 00:01:39.441
Whenever Patrasche tired from the heavy load, his master beat him without mercy.

14
00:01:39.442 --> 00:01:45.888
One day, Patrasche was pulling the cart along a straight, dusty road.

15
00:01:45.889 --> 00:01:54.152
They were heading to the lovely city of Antwerp, where the great painter Rubens once lived.

16
00:01:54.153 --> 00:02:00.130
The merchant walked lazily beside the cart, smoking a pipe.

17
00:02:00.131 --> 00:02:04.186
It was the middle of summer and very warm.

18
00:02:04.187 --> 00:02:09.512
Patrasche's master stopped at every wine shop along the way.

19
00:02:09.513 --> 00:02:19.787
But poor Patrasche─his master never unhooked him from the cart or gave him a drink of water.

20
00:02:19.788 --> 00:02:27.204
Patrasche was exhausted, but he was a hardworking dog and kept walking.

21
00:02:27.205 --> 00:02:32.669
When he started to slow down, his master hit him with a whip.

22
00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:39.630
"Patrasche, you stupid dog, go faster!" he yelled.

23
00:02:39.631 --> 00:02:43.337
The whip stung Patrasche's side.

24
00:02:43.338 --> 00:02:48.348
His eyes were full of dust and his muscles ached.

25
00:02:48.349 --> 00:02:52.615
"Lazy beast," yelled his master.

26
00:02:52.616 --> 00:02:55.345
"We must reach Antwerp tonight.

27
00:02:55.346 --> 00:02:58.855
There is a market there tomorrow."

28
00:02:58.856 --> 00:03:05.694
Still Patrasche pulled with his tired legs, but the effort was too much.

29
00:03:05.695 --> 00:03:09.788
He staggered and fell over on his side.

30
00:03:09.789 --> 00:03:19.019
He lay in the middle of the dusty road, motionless in the full heat of the sun.

31
00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:28.628
Patrasche's cruel master gave him his usual medicine─a kick in the ribs and a lash from his whip.

32
00:03:28.629 --> 00:03:40.898
Patrasche had received beatings many times before but this time he did not move or cry.

33
00:03:40.899 --> 00:03:44.204
The merchant cursed Patrasche.

34
00:03:44.205 --> 00:03:48.267
"Get up, you lazy dog!" he shouted.

35
00:03:48.268 --> 00:03:51.720
"We've still got three miles to walk!"

36
00:03:51.721 --> 00:03:54.802
But Patrasche did not hear him.

37
00:03:54.803 --> 00:04:00.157
He was too tired even to feel his master's blows.

38
00:04:00.158 --> 00:04:04.095
The merchant cursed the poor dog again.

39
00:04:04.096 --> 00:04:08.653
He kicked his body off the road and into the grass.

40
00:04:08.654 --> 00:04:13.145
Patrasche's master lifted the front of the cart.

41
00:04:13.146 --> 00:04:19.098
With a frown and a moan, he began to pull it down the road.

42
00:04:19.099 --> 00:04:26.887
For two long, cruel years, Patrasche had worked for his master from morning to night.

43
00:04:26.888 --> 00:04:30.114
He had been loyal and strong.

44
00:04:30.115 --> 00:04:34.017
But the merchant had forgotten him already.

45
00:04:34.018 --> 00:04:41.953
Muttering to himself as he pulled his cart, the merchant left Patrasche to die.

46
00:04:41.954 --> 00:04:50.271
"When I reach the next village, I'll steal the first dog I see wandering around," the merchant thought.

47
00:04:50.272 --> 00:04:53.889
He kept walking and didn't look back.

48
00:04:53.890 --> 00:05:08.007
Poor Patrasche was too exhausted even to lift his head and moan.
